1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,300
Every agent you pay for is capable, and none of them

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:07,500
share your tools, your memory, or your judgement.

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:11,100
And when an agent says a job is done,

4
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:15,200
that is a claim, not a fact. This is Cherry.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,900
I give Cherry an outcome. It returns a bounded plan:

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,700
tasks with dependencies, a definition of done,

7
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:27,500
and a real check on every one.

8
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:30,700
That is why this use case fits WebMCP.

9
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:33,900
Planning work is a page with live state,

10
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,100
and a visiting agent should drive that state

11
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:41,200
through the site's own tools instead of guessing at buttons.

12
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,700
Here is the gate. Site tools let an agent create a mission,

13
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:54,700
plan it, start it, cancel it, and request a decision.

14
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:58,700
None of them can approve. That is the better experience:

15
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:01,900
I stop typing prompts and start reading decisions,

16
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,400
and every agent call lands in a log I can read.

17
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,100
How it is implemented: the page calls document.modelContext.registerTool.

18
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:16,700
Seven tools are always on, six reads plus introduce_agent,

19
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:21,500
which only labels the session, and at most five more per surface,

20
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:23,900
registered and retired as state changes.

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,900
This browser has no WebMCP host, so nothing is registered,

22
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,000
and the page says so.

23
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,900
This is a real Codex run, committed to the repository

24
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,500
and replayed here from evidence checked against its fingerprint.

25
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:45,500
Two agents, thirty-four and a half seconds of measured overlap,

26
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:47,900
each in its own git worktree.

27
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,900
The boundary is a worktree process, never a virtual machine.

28
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:53,900
Completion came from the host.

29
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,800
Success came from the runner's own checks.

30
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,100
Most agent-ready sites let an agent operate them.

31
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:07,100
Cherry's site upgrades the agent.

32
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:10,300
recommend_skills hands a visiting agent my approved methods,

33
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:14,700
pinned to the revision I read, with a hash it verifies.

34
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:18,700
People and agents could not build one library together before.

35
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:22,800
Now they can, and only I approve what enters it.

36
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,300
Every claim is labelled by the test or capture behind it.

37
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:34,700
A live browser host is still Experimental, and we say so.

38
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:37,200
No API key. MIT licensed. Cherry.
